## Profile a single write

**ptrace is OPEN (verified Sep 1 2026 by live attach on all 15 hosts):**
`kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0` on node9901, fstor007 and fcstor003–015, so
`strace -p`, `gdb -p`, `vgdb` and LSan all work same-uid. It is **runtime
only** (`sysctl -w`); `/etc/sysctl.d/99-ptrace.conf` still says 2, so it
reverts on reboot — ask the USER to re-apply, do not edit the conf.
**Kernel-side profiling is OPEN as of Sep 19 2026 (user ran
`sysctl -w kernel.perf_event_paranoid=1 kernel.kptr_restrict=0` via clush on
fcstor003–015, node9901, fstor007):** `perf record -g -p <pid>` with kernel
symbols works same-uid; **`perf record -a` is refused at paranoid=1**
(checked Oct 1 — attach `-p` after the daemon's pools exist). Runtime
only (`sysctl -w`) like ptrace_scope — reverts on reboot; ask the USER to
re-apply, do not edit `/etc/sysctl.d`. `/sys/kernel/tracing/events` may
still be unreadable (that is tracefs perms, not paranoid).
Still do NOT restart daemons under strace — the 353 MiB/s
`Disk stats: md0` run was local RAID after the daemons were killed, not a
tracing overhead measurement. Attach to a RUNNING daemon instead. Same-uid:

```
perf record -e cpu-clock -g --call-graph fp -p $(pgrep -x efsd) -o /tmp/perf-efsd.data sleep 20
perf stat -p $(pgrep -x efsd) sleep 20
# same for efs-fuse; then:
timeout 25 perf report --stdio --no-children -n -i /tmp/perf-efsd.data
```

What the profiles show (Sep 17–18, and the same on the old engine): the
client's on-CPU time is blake3 in `hash_write_fragments` /
`dcache_flush_slot_inner`, the server is near-idle, and the wall is
off-CPU — `fsync`/REPORT wait, not hashing. Optimizing blake3 does not
move the number; splitting the fsync tail is the lever (W3).
